      "id": 1,
      "text": "Youngsville began as El Rito Encinos, a 1800s sheep‑and‑goat village granted land by the Spanish crown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23,
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.thegrandhacienda.com/post/youngsville"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Land grants in Youngsville sparked lawsuits and fraud, prompting U.S. courts to confirm ownership after New Mexico became a U.S. territory.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.thegrandhacienda.com/post/youngsville"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Unlike most towns, Youngsville residents drive to a local post office to pick up mail and chat with Veronica behind the desk.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.thegrandhacienda.com/post/youngsville"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The winding road past Youngsville skirts Cerro Pedernal, a 9,862‑foot flint hill that inspired many of Georgia O’Keeffe’s paintings.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.thegrandhacienda.com/post/youngsville"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In Youngsville, Spanish still dominates street conversations, reflecting the town’s enduring Spanish‑colonial roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.thegrandhacienda.com/post/youngsville"
    },
    {
